# Packages

A site installs `mdsvex`. Its entries are `mdsvex`, the Vite plugin, and [`mdsvex/compile`](/docs/reference/compile), [`mdsvex/highlight`](/docs/reference/highlighting#highlighting-with-compile), [`mdsvex/math`](/docs/reference/math#math-with-compile), [`mdsvex/host`](/docs/guides/without-vite) and the [`mdsvex/globals`](/docs/guides/frontmatter#typescript) types.

The packages below sit under it and install on their own. The entries in these tables are the surface to build on. Anything else they export, a name with a leading underscore or one marked internal, is there for mdsvex and its tools and changes without notice.

## Parser and renderer

| package and entry | export | what for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `@mdsvex/parse` | `parse_markdown_svelte(source, options)` | a batch parse, into a `NodeBuffer` |
| `@mdsvex/parse` | `PFMParser` | an incremental parse, `init`, `feed` and `finish`, or `parse` for a whole string. It calls an `Emitter` with what it finds |
| `@mdsvex/parse` | `ParseStallError` | a parse that stops moving |
| `@mdsvex/parse/tree-builder` | `TreeBuilder` | the `Emitter` that builds a `NodeBuffer` |
| `@mdsvex/parse/cursor` | `Cursor` | walks a `NodeBuffer` |
| `@mdsvex/render` | `CursorHTMLRenderer` | the HTML string renderer `compile` uses |
| `@mdsvex/render` and `@mdsvex/render/Node.svelte` | `ComponentRenderer` and the `Node` component | renders a buffer live in Svelte, as it grows |
| `@mdsvex/render/stream` | `PluginStream`, `PLUGIN_DATA` | runs plugins over a stream, and gives components the plugin data as it changes |
| `@mdsvex/render/sourcemap` | `mappings_to_v3` | a v3 source map from the `mappings` of a `compile` with `sourcemap: true` |

- **Node kinds are types only.** `NodeKind`, `WireOp` and `StateKind` from `@mdsvex/parse`, and `NodeKind` and `NodeField` from `@mdsvex/parse/utils`, are `const enum`s, which have no value at runtime. A named import of one fails in JavaScript, and TypeScript with `isolatedModules` reports TS2748. At runtime, `string_to_kind('heading')` and `kind_to_string(kind)` from `@mdsvex/parse/utils` turn a kind into its number and back.
- **Parse plugins** are written against the types `mdsvex` exports, see [parse plugins](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins). [`PLUGINS.md`][plugins-md] in `@mdsvex/parse` has the whole API.
- **Source offsets** are string indices, in UTF-16 code units, into the source with its line endings made `\n`.
- **Braces in code** come out of `CursorHTMLRenderer` as `&#123;` and `&#125;`, since Svelte reads a bare brace as an expression. For HTML that Svelte never compiles, set `renderer.code_braces = false` before `update`. Plain fences and code spans then keep their braces as written, and a document with a lot of code renders faster. A highlighter still escapes its own output.
- **A fence in a stream is highlighted** when the `Node` component gets a `highlight` prop. It takes what the [`highlight` option](/docs/reference/highlighting#highlighting-with-compile) of `compile` takes, a function from code to HTML or what `create_highlight` returns, and writes the same markup as a compiled page, so one stylesheet styles both. Pass it to every `Node`, as `<Node {buf} idx={block.idx} source="" {highlight} />`.
  - The highlighter is synchronous and its HTML goes to the page as it is, so it has to escape the code. `null` draws the fence plain, as a highlighter that throws does. A function is told `open: true` while the fence is still being written.
  - A `create_highlight` config knows a fence is open. A comment that may still become an annotation, as `// [!code` is, is not drawn until it is one or is not, and a line the meta names, as `{4-6}` does, is marked once it arrives.
  - An open fence is highlighted again on every chunk until it is 4,096 characters long. Past that it is plain until it closes, and is then highlighted once, so a long fence costs no more to stream than a plain one.
  - Languages that load later need nothing else. Leave the prop `undefined` until they are there, then set it: the fences already on the page are highlighted where they stand, and no other block renders again. A highlighter that reads `$state` does the same when that state changes.
  - A code span with a `#!` hint, as `` `#!js let a` `` is, goes through the same highlighter, with `inline: true`. A span with no hint stays plain.
  - The live expressions of an `eval` fence show as text.
- **Math in a stream is rendered** when the `Node` component gets a `math` prop. It takes what the [`math` option](/docs/reference/math#math-with-compile) of `compile` takes, a function from LaTeX to HTML such as `load_math` from `mdsvex/math` gives, and writes the same markup as a compiled page. Pass it to every `Node`, as `<Node {buf} idx={block.idx} source="" {math} />`.
  - A `math` fence is rendered while it is still being written. It shows as its LaTeX, in the code block it is without the prop, until the LaTeX so far first renders. From then on, LaTeX the renderer throws on keeps the last render on the page, so an equation grows a term at a time and holds still through a half written `\frac`. An environment such as `aligned` renders nothing until its `\end`, so it shows as LaTeX until then.
  - An open fence is rendered again on every chunk until it is 4,096 characters long. Past that it keeps its last render until it closes.
  - Inline math shows nothing until its closing dollar, then is rendered once.
  - A closed fence or span the renderer throws on is drawn as written and nothing is reported, since a stream has no build to fail. To show an error in its place, catch it in the renderer and return the markup for it.
  - The renderer is synchronous and its HTML goes to the page as it is. `load_math` is not, so leave the prop `undefined` until it resolves, then set it: the math already on the page is rendered where it stands.
  - With the prop set, a `math` fence and a code span with a `#!math` hint never reach the highlighter, as in a compile.
- **Components replace elements in a stream** when the `Node` component gets a `components` prop, an object from an element name to a component, as `{ h2: Heading, pre: CodeBlock }`. A replacement gets [the props it gets in a compiled document](/docs/guides/components#element-props), so one component serves both. Pass it to every `Node`.
  - The elements markdown makes are replaced, and those a plugin makes, and the parts of a table. So is lowercase HTML in the source, unless an element has a directive such as `bind:`. `component_mode="markdown"` keeps HTML in the source as plain elements, as [`component_mode`](/docs/guides/components#component_mode-all) does by default for a compiled document.
  - A replacement is drawn as soon as its node opens, with the props and the children that have arrived. A link whose definition comes later has no `href` until then, and the `code` of a `pre` grows.
  - A block that changes is drawn again from nothing when it is keyed by its `v`, so a replacement in an open block is made again on each chunk. State it keeps lasts only once its block has closed.
  - A `pre` replacement gets `lang`, `meta` and `code`, and the `<pre>` as `children`, highlighted when there is a `highlight` prop. The `title`, `caption` and meta props of a compiled document are not passed.
  - A `math` replacement gets math once it has rendered, with `code`, `display` for a fence, `meta`, and the rendered math as `children`, which grows while a fence is open. Math that has not rendered yet, or that the renderer throws on, is a plain code block and goes to no replacement. With no `math` prop, math is code and goes to `pre` and `code`.
  - `footnote` and `footnotes` are not replaced in a stream.
  - A stream runs no `<script>`. A component the document imports, an expression and a block such as `{#each}` draw nothing.
- **Footnotes in a stream** are numbered and collected as on a compiled page when every `Node` gets the `footnotes` of the `ComponentRenderer`, as `<Node {buf} idx={block.idx} source="" footnotes={renderer.footnotes} />`. Read `renderer.footnotes` after each `update`, it is a new object whenever the document has a footnote.
  - A reference is numbered and linked as soon as it arrives, in the order of first reference, and a definition is not drawn where it was written.
  - The notes are drawn in a `<section class="footnotes">` by one more entry at the end of `renderer.blocks`, which the same `Node` draws, so the each loop needs no other change. The section appears with the first note that has both a reference and a definition.
  - The ids and links are those of a compiled page, so one stylesheet styles both and a link to a note works in either.
- **The wire format**, `WireEmitter` and `WireTreeBuilder`, sends a parse to another process as opcodes. It is not a stable surface yet. [`WIRE_FORMAT.md`][wire-md] describes it as it is now.

## Editor tools

| package | how it is used | what for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `@mdsvex/language-server` | the `pfm-language-server` command, with `--stdio` | the language server for any editor that speaks LSP. The VS Code and Zed extensions run it |
| `@mdsvex/typescript-plugin` | `"plugins": [{ "name": "@mdsvex/typescript-plugin" }]` in `compilerOptions` | lets the TypeScript server read `.svx` imports in `.ts` and `.svelte` files |
| `@mdsvex/prettier-plugin` | a Prettier plugin | formats documents, see [Prettier](/docs/guides/prettier) |

`@mdsvex/language-core` and `@mdsvex/source-map` are the pieces the language server and the TypeScript plugin are built from, and have no surface of their own to build on. [`ARCHITECTURE.md`][architecture] explains how they fit together.
